WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.726 --> 00:00:04.143
(light orchestral music)

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.070
- We need to look at something astounding

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.770
that happened over the course
of like a hundred years.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.050
This is the final chapter,

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.870
and what I think is the
most mind-boggling chapter,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.880
in the story of how Europe
took over the world.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.200
The reason why it's so mind-boggling

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.820
is because this is the part of the story

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.610
where the map goes from
looking like this in 1800

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.310
with Europe controlling like
35% of the world's land,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.740
to looking like this by 1914.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.157
(light orchestral music)

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.160
With 84% of the Earth being
controlled by the people

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.690
or the descendants of the people

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.810
from this once isolated
continent in Europe.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.903
84%, how on Earth did this happen?

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.030
A huge part of this next chapter

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.700
has to do with this continent,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.110
the second largest continent on Earth

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.850
and the part of the
world that the Europeans

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.250
hadn't really carved up yet.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.580
So this is where the whole
story comes together.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.370
It shows us how technology
and different ways of thinking

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.410
helped these people take over the world,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.410
and in the process, shaped
the world we live in today.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.230
The way that we trade,
where we get our stuff,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.600
the language I'm using to
speak to you right now,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.860
and most of you understand it

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.170
even though you don't live
anywhere near the place

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.040
where it was invented.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.710
I'm telling you, this isn't
far away history anymore.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.450
This is the world we currently live in.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.910
So let me show you the
third and final chapter

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.503
of how Europe stole the world.

00:01:29.629 --> 00:01:32.546
(orchestral music)

00:01:39.743 --> 00:01:43.650
Another video, another set
of beautiful printed maps.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:44.483
And I'm telling you,

00:01:44.483 --> 00:01:46.560
this is the chapter where
the maps get really good.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.000
Cartography really took off.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.710
Okay, let's get up to
speed on where we're at.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.930
Remember it all started
with Spain and Portugal.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.660
They ramped up this colonialism
thing back around 1500

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.060
and this led them to divide
the world between them

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.640
until other European
countries got in on it, too.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.230
Then the real competition started.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.970
The Dutch created the modern corporation,

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.130
which allowed them to
speed all of this up.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.410
The world quickly turned
into a giant marketplace

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.170
run by Europeans with boats and guns

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.330
and incentives to bring profits

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.122
to the shareholders back home.

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:16.560
(playful music)

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.060
This is all a huge part of the story,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.790
but I'm telling you,
when it comes to scale,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.250
imperialism is just getting started

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.620
and that's because Europeans
are about to level up.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.780
We're gonna call this Imperialism 2.0,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.670
a new way of taking over the world,

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.300
fueled mostly by technology
and a rare cooperation

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.893
between all of these empires.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.800
One British prime minister described

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.000
this Imperialism 2.0 as,

00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:42.390
"the vulgar and bastard imperialism

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.400
of irritation and aggression,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.427
of grabbing everything even
if we had no use for it."

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.160
But let me tell you, if you're like me

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.050
and you kind of have a
low key implicit belief

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.000
that European domination was inevitable

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.590
and that this was gonna
happen no matter what,

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.530
I'm here to tell you that
it almost didn't happen.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.750
That's because by the end of the 1700s

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.810
revolution was in the air.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.227
(light orchestral music)

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.050
Empires were losing their colonies,

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.730
starting with a group of Europeans

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.580
who were done having a king
and declaring independence

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.440
for themselves in the late 1700s.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.420
Soon you had a bunch of Spanish colonies

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.830
declaring independence.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.140
And then over here in Haiti
you had enslaved people

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.970
who were organizing and rebelling

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.590
against their French masters,

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.780
throwing them out and
starting their own country.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.030
These empires weren't only
losing their colonies.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.480
Back in Europe, one ruler even
lost his head in all of this.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.520
The empires were losing their grip

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.710
and soon they were
fighting with one another

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:38.730
like never before.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.010
It was chaos and it totally freaked

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.390
these European rulers out.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:43.920
Are they losing their empire?

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.930
Are they gonna lose their reigns on power?

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.840
Is the era of abundance and
domination coming to an end?

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.240
No, we can't let this happen.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.850
So they start doing something
that was kind of unheard of.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.190
Instead of fighting and
competing with each other,

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.270
like they've always done,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.580
the European powers start
talking to each other.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:00.870
Their empires were in jeopardy

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.400
and they needed to collaborate,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.740
find ways to share power
both at home in Europe,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.270
but also on the world stage.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.240
Soon, this new culture of
diplomacy and collaboration

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.580
would turn to focus on the one continent

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.740
that none of these European
powers had carved up yet.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.390
The new imperial frontier.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.920
Certainly full of resources,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.947
but not yet conquered.

00:04:18.947 --> 00:04:21.780
(whimsical music)

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.910
I mean, the maps tell the story here.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.000
The maps were like a record
of what Europeans knew

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.440
and didn't know about the world.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.410
I mean, this one British
map from 1800s says it all.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.243
Look at this thing.

00:04:38.243 --> 00:04:40.110
Europeans were definitely
familiar with Africa,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.030
especially here in the coast,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.040
where for hundreds of years
they had trading posts,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.320
and of course, the Atlantic slave trade.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.020
But look how they mapped the
interior of the continent.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.460
It literally just gives up

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.000
and is like inland parts
almost entirely unknown,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.350
which is pretty rare for this time period.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.420
At this point, the
Europeans had really mapped

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.253
a lot of the world,

00:04:58.253 --> 00:04:59.520
but this place was off limits.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.220
It was the stuff of legend, of myths.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.020
The caption here on the map says that

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.290
this interior part of
Africa "may be considered

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.740
as absolutely unknown or
completely unexplored.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.020
All we know," says the map, "is that

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.020
its immense and arid sands are intersected

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.680
with complete collections
of the most ferocious beasts

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.200
and most uncivilized men."

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.708
That's all they know.

00:05:23.708 --> 00:05:26.208
(light music)

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.240
There was a very good reason for this.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.300
The fact is that soldiers
and explorers from Europe

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.740
who went into this area,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.670
a lot of 'em didn't come home.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.040
Up to 40% died from diseases

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.320
like the mosquito-borne illness, malaria.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.960
So much so that this part of the map

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.360
became known as the White Man's Grave,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.620
totally off limits.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.490
But that soon changed.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.157
(playful music)

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.370
Two giant things happen
that change everything,

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.560
completely redirect what Europeans can do

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.940
with their mounting power.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.980
First, these two French guys are able

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.480
to take the bark of this tree,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:07.800
which had been used for a very long time

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.850
for a variety of purposes

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.610
and isolate a vital
chemical called quinine.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.300
It combats malaria, the major
killer of Europeans in Africa.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.310
They now have a white man's shield

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.800
to protect them from
the White Man's Grave.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.050
The door is slowly creaking open.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.450
Second, this guy happens.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.420
King Leopold II, he's the
king of this new country

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.820
called Belgium, and it
existed for like 40 years.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.290
It's kind of a minor place in Europe,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.330
nothing like these OG colonizers.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.420
So it's like 1875, and
King Leopold wants to play

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.290
with the big boys.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:38.610
He wants a colony.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.770
So he literally goes
around and starts asking

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.320
these major colonial powers
for like some of their land.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.570
Like he goes to the
OGs, Spain and Portugal,

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.070
and he's like, "Hey guys,
I know you're in decline.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.600
Can I have one of your colonies?"

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:49.710
And they're like, "No."

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.007
So then he goes to the
British and he's like,

00:06:51.007 --> 00:06:52.740
"Hey guys, I know you have New Guinea,

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.030
maybe you don't have any plans for it.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.170
You could give it to me."

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.850
And they're like, "No, Leo.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.160
Don't you realize how hard
we worked to steal this land?

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.600
We're not gonna give it
to you as a handout."

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.570
So King Leopold decides to take
matters into his own hands.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.690
He turns to the map and
decides this part of the map,

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.940
the parts unknown, where
none of the colonizers

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.240
have arrived to yet, is a
prime place for his colony.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.360
King Leopold sets his sight
on the White Man's Grave.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.460
A quick reminder that this

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.860
is kind of the fantasy of the Europeans

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:20.700
who haven't explored this.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.050
In reality, at this point,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.120
Africa looks a lot more like this.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.370
But in the mind of King
Leopold and other Europeans,

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.273
it's a big, blank canvas
with unlimited possibilities.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.550
So Leopold sends explorers
to like the dead center

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.050
of this blank canvas.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.700
And they're armed, not only with some

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.110
of the latest and greatest weapons,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.120
but also with medicine that shields them

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.130
from these killer tropical diseases.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.690
These Belgian explorers arrive

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.480
and are able to make
agreements with the locals,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.130
laying claimed to this land.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.350
King Leopold now has his own little colony

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.300
in the center of the White Man's Grave.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.393
And of course, they start mapping it.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.220
This is a map from a bunch

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.350
of Belgian cartographers and explorers

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.830
when they first arrive to
this center part of Africa.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.540
Very little detail here at the beginning.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:10.770
This is 1880.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.330
They basically got this river,

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.230
some of the offshoots,

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:16.230
but they don't really know
what's going on in here yet.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.987
This becomes the frame that Leopold uses

00:08:18.987 --> 00:08:20.868
to build his colony.

00:08:20.868 --> 00:08:24.150
(light music)

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.070
Now of course, this freaks the French out

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.100
because they're like, "Hey,
what's Belgium doing in Africa?

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.690
Why are they exploring all this land?"

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.700
So they decide to send their own explorers

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.290
to claim their own bit of land.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:35.730
After all, they've got the medicine,

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.990
it's not nearly as dangerous,

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.220
and the Belgians are doing it.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.170
Well, of course now the
British are waking up.

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.490
They're sending people, too.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.890
And even the new kid
on the imperial block,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.300
Germany is chipping in,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.812
and now suddenly we've got
a scramble on our hands.

00:08:48.812 --> 00:08:52.229
(tense orchestral music)

00:08:53.227 --> 00:08:54.877
"But wait," say the European powers,

00:08:54.877 --> 00:08:56.310
"Let's learn from our mistakes.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.450
Instead of the old days where we always

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:58.680
had to fight over things,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.020
in this era of revolution and warfare,

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.520
remember that we're trying to be better

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:03.960
about talking to one another?

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:05.850
Coordinating, remember?"

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.650
So it's 1884 and all these big,

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.920
Africa hungry European empires
get together in Berlin.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.360
I mean, there's amazing painting

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.800
of just them all sitting here,

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.660
looking at this big beautiful map,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.090
which would be like an
amazing activity to do

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.890
until you realize what's
actually happening here.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.480
You've got the chancellor of Germany,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.370
you've got the OG colonizers,

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.200
Portugal explaining this place

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.120
to Belgium and France and Italy,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.280
and you've even got the new
country, the United States

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.860
who showed up kind of new
to all this imperial stuff,

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.440
but quickly learning
how power really works

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.610
on the international stage.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.590
Basically, anyone in
Europe who didn't have

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:39.630
an empire yet got in now.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:43.110
Oh, and crucially, they didn't
invite any African leaders.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.540
And to be clear, this painting
isn't like exaggerated,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.860
like it's not a political cartoon.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.200
This is literally a
bunch of European dudes

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.200
in a room in Berlin in 1884

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.020
discussing and coordinating
how they're going to carve up

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.520
and take this continent.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.770
And they decided that there
was gonna be one big rule

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.360
for this new scramble for Africa.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.260
No pretending.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.230
None of this only on the
map, fake imperialism thing

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.240
that the Pope arranged
for Spain and Portugal

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.590
a few hundred years previous.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.570
You actually have to control the land

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:10.500
if you're gonna claim it.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.540
So they divide up the
map on who gets what,

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.190
they leave the conference,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:15.240
and they get to work.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.660
The French start moving in
quickly from West Africa.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.520
The British begin taking over Sudan

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.130
and expanding north from
down here in South Africa

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:24.720
to take over all of this land.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.490
The Germans really start
establishing themselves

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:27.900
here and over here.

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.430
Italy starts occupying
all this land up here

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:30.780
in the north and east.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.970
And Leopold, well, he got his colony.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.830
77 times the size of Belgium,

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.270
here it was as a blank canvas

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.880
when they first started exploring in 1880.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.830
Here it is 14 years later.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.800
Little Belgium down here,
giant Congo up here.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.100
The blueprint is filled out,
the conquest is complete.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.410
With his new colony,
King Leopold, of course,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.700
went on to do horrible things,

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.370
exploiting, pillaging the
resources from this place

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.440
and wreaking horrible havoc on the people.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.270
And it is horrific and it is horrendous,

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:58.560
and I made a whole other video

00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:59.640
that kind of goes into it more.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.800
I'll link to it and go to the sources

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:02.880
in the description, please.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:06.750
Okay.
(intense orchestral music)

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.540
All of these empires
were carving up this map,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.550
coming in, mapping it very beautifully.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.440
It's like literally the
opposite of what it was.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.210
It's unknown and now it's totally known.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:16.920
Pillaging the resources,
bringing it back home,

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.110
making record profits,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.940
And all of this, this carving,

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.912
it happened really fast.

00:11:22.912 --> 00:11:26.495
(intense orchestral music)

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:32.970
But wait a minute, hold on, pause.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.580
How does this make any sense?

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.650
Like it made sense when
it was all like on water

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:39.150
and there were ships
and there was domination

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.650
and trading ports and all of that,

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.990
but this, this is an incredibly,

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.660
logistically ambitious thing to do.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.400
Like this was actually a
central question for me

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.200
that led me to make this series

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.990
because I just didn't
understand how these countries,

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.610
in a matter of a few years,

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:57.420
could completely carve up
the second largest continent?

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:58.830
Well, the answer to that question

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.120
isn't that surprising.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:01.920
These Europeans now had a leg up.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.441
They had new tools.

00:12:03.441 --> 00:12:05.760
(upbeat music)

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.370
Remember they had invented
capitalism to make them rich.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.410
That gave them time to do science,

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:12.330
which gave them technology that they used

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.090
to make their capitalism
better and more effective,

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:16.110
more productive.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:19.080
This cycle repeated itself
over and over and over,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.850
giving Europeans a further

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.920
and further leg up technologically,

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:26.100
until soon they had stuff
like this, a steamboat.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.660
You didn't have to worry
about the wind anymore

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:28.493
to keep going.

00:12:28.493 --> 00:12:31.182
You could just steam your way
all the way up African rivers.

00:12:31.182 --> 00:12:32.177
- Or the old African queen.

00:12:32.177 --> 00:12:35.970
- Or the railroad, quick way
to transport food and troops.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.080
Like you can see this map,

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:39.960
all of this red is either
railroads that they put in

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.330
or railroads that they were
constructing at this time.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:43.800
This allowed Europeans to level up,

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.780
not just in Africa, but everywhere.

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:47.760
I mean, here they are in India.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.910
The British quickly taking
over this entire subcontinent

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:53.460
of what today's India and
Pakistan and Bangladesh

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.040
with this massive complex rail system

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.470
that they built basically in no time.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.240
They also invented the telegraph,

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.640
which could now relay messages
in a matter of minutes

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:03.840
instead of weeks.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.120
I mean, this political
cartoon really personifies

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:07.620
how powerful this was.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.547
And of course, what we've been
looking at this whole time.

00:13:10.547 --> 00:13:13.130
(upbeat music)

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:16.932
They made maps.

00:13:16.932 --> 00:13:17.910
(orchestral music)

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.910
Big, beautiful juicy maps
showing the geography

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:24.270
and the people and all of the
land that they had conquered.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:25.500
In addition to technology,

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.450
these empires had also perfected the art

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.610
of allying with local power holders

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:31.627
and turning the people against each other.

00:13:31.627 --> 00:13:32.850
"Divide and conquer,"

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.220
which allowed a small group of Europeans

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.290
to control millions of locals.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:40.465
And of course, they had these.

00:13:40.465 --> 00:13:43.382
(orchestral music)

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.400
These refined killing machines

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.560
that allowed small groups
of European soldiers

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.710
to rip through truly
formidable African armies.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.540
Like look at this painting from Sudan

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:56.100
where the British used their guns

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.580
to slaughter 10,000 enemies
with just a few hundred losses.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:01.590
And hear this casual caption

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:03.187
showing that these savages were now

00:14:03.187 --> 00:14:06.000
"mowed down by these
modern weapons of war,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.307
clearing way for civilization."

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:12.450
Now, it wasn't this easy everywhere.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:13.980
Descendants of white Dutch settlers

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.630
held off the British for a long time

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:16.800
down here in South Africa,

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:19.110
and the Ethiopians were able
to hold off the Italians

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:20.310
from conquering their land,

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.910
making it the only place in
Africa to never be colonized.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:25.890
But listen, in the midst
of all this bloodshed,

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:28.230
we have to talk about something
that doesn't fit cleanly

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.000
into our narrative of good versus evil.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.490
Because the presence of these
Europeans in this continent

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.410
also brought really positive things.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:36.000
Remember those French
dudes that discovered

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.140
the treatment for malaria?

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.700
Well that was tested in the field

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.740
in French Algeria, a colony.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.660
It changed medicine forever,

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.300
giving us our modern
understanding of mosquitoes

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.430
and the diseases they spread.

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:50.010
This scramble into Africa
helped push forward

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.100
our understanding of health
and disease and medicine.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:55.530
These and tons of other
medical developments

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.660
help these Europeans conquer land,

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.440
but it also brought innovation
that we still use today

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.933
that has saved countless lives.

00:15:03.933 --> 00:15:06.433
(light music)

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.580
Okay, so technology was
a major defining factor,

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:12.840
but it wasn't just technology.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.520
Once again, we see in this chapter

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:15.750
what we saw in other chapters.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:17.670
That Europeans had to develop new,

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.890
sophisticated mental inventions

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.600
that allowed this all to go down.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.520
The popular story that they
were telling themselves

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.070
at this time was that all civilization

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.890
could be ranked according
to the level of development.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.660
And look, according to this analysis,

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.280
they placed themselves at the top

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:33.900
and they could tell themselves very easily

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.030
that they were the enlightened
people of the world.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.710
This new colonizing story

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.380
was the most sophisticated
and tantalizing yet,

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:41.820
and it's one that's still kind of embedded

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.020
in a lot of our brains still.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:45.480
That the enlightened
civilization had a burden

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.000
to bring civilization to
the rest of the world.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.890
And for that, they kind of needed

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.600
to stay on top and control.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:52.560
And the reason why this story

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.030
was so believable and tantalizing

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.530
is because at this time

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:59.280
it was being blended with
actual real objective science

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:00.780
that was being done by Europeans.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.580
Like this guy, Charles Darwin,

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:04.200
someone who changed the way that we think

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.370
about the natural world.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.770
He had just put out a book
about how animals evolved

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.590
into hierarchies with different
capabilities and traits.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:12.960
Well, if that applies to all animals,

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.510
then it must apply to humans themselves

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.730
and their societies and
their civilizations.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.650
So then they go out into
the field with their maps

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.180
and they start gathering observations

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:22.920
that confirm this story.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.810
And soon, they're
measuring people's skulls

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:25.830
all around the world.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:26.910
They're keeping notes,

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:28.830
they're developing theories and terms,

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.270
they're writing academic papers,

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:32.850
all of this to define a pretend set

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:34.770
of pseudo-scientific ideas,

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:37.290
the idea that we're all
part of a different race,

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:39.750
all with different natural capabilities.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:42.450
And that is what must explain

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:45.180
why some people have the
resources and the technology

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.350
and others do not.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.170
Like the previous stories that
Europeans told themselves,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:50.970
this one was intoxicating.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.920
Think of all the generations that passed

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:54.330
where this story could be ingrained

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.280
into the minds of the people.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.380
But again, remember that I'm not saying

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.150
that these Europeans
are telling themselves

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:01.200
this story every day.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:02.580
We're now talking about the great, great,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:05.550
great grandchildren of like
the original colonizers.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.760
The individual people didn't
have the grand plan in mind

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.110
to go carve up Africa.

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:12.180
They were just responding
to what they knew,

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:13.860
what they'd been told was real,

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:15.780
what they wanted to believe.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.250
It was a way of life.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:18.930
It was a way of thinking.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:20.670
And if we think that we're somehow exempt

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:23.130
from a similar type of mental
model that we don't see

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:25.170
but that dictates our behavior,

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:26.130
we're tricking ourselves.

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.810
I mean, listen to one of
these British imperialists,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.160
Cecil Rhodes.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:31.860
He says, "We happen to be
the best people in the world

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.140
with the highest ideals
of decency and justice,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:35.400
liberty and peace.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.350
And the more of the world we inhabit,

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.570
the better it is for humanity."

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.599
Okay, Cecil has made up his mind.

00:17:42.599 --> 00:17:43.650
(orchestral music)

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:45.270
Okay, so let's look at the map

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:47.130
in like the early 1900s.

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:48.660
Africa looks like this,

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.030
completely carved up by European powers.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.560
Over here, the Dutch had conquered

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:54.330
the entire Indonesian archipelago.

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:55.860
The French completely taking over

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.300
this part of Southeast Asia.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.030
But the real kingpin in all
of this taking over land stuff

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:01.350
was the British Empire.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.660
In addition to all this
stuff they had in Africa,

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.510
they occupied the huge
Indian subcontinent.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:08.310
They also had a few of
these important ports

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:10.080
like Hong Kong and Singapore.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.360
I mean, I can't go over all
of the stuff they took over

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.440
'cause it's just too much.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:14.280
At the peak of their empire,

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.560
they ruled over 412 million people,

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.300
which was a ton for that time.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:21.690
Their domination had spread
to almost 25% of the globe,

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:25.320
making Britain this rainy
set of islands in Europe

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:27.780
the biggest empire that ever existed.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:29.670
In doing so, they spread their people,

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.770
their ideas, their economic system,

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:34.680
their fringe language to
every corner of the world,

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:36.030
including where I'm sitting right now.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.160
Because remember, the US
is just one expression

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:39.120
of the British Empire,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:40.620
the branch of the empire that went on

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.420
to become the most powerful
country in the world

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.000
to influence how the
world order would look.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:49.436
By 1914, Europe had successfully
taken over the world.

00:18:49.436 --> 00:18:51.270
(light music)

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.640
They were deathly rich compared
to the rest of the globe.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:56.220
And their ideas, both good and bad,

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.650
were deeply embedded in
the international system.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.540
But suddenly, all of this technology,

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:03.450
all this industrialization
that made them so effective,

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:05.820
turned away from conquering faraway lands

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.929
and was turned on each other.

00:19:07.929 --> 00:19:09.158
(cannon explodes)

00:19:09.158 --> 00:19:11.070
(orchestral music)

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.650
Over the next 30 years,
hundreds of millions of people

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:16.860
are killed in the two
most destructive wars ever

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:18.660
made possible by all the same things

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:20.790
that allow Europeans
to take over the world,

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:22.920
sophisticated weapons in technology

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.140
that Europeans are now
turning on each other.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.300
The so-called sophisticated race

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.100
is now slaughtering one another

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.930
on an unprecedented scale.

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:32.700
These wars didn't do the image

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.070
of the civilized Europeans any good.

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:38.040
And Western-schooled
local elites decided that

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:41.040
they didn't wanna be ruled
by foreign forces anymore.

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.220
They were able to rally their
people around common language

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:46.950
and birth to national
identity that didn't include

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.440
being ruled by white people
from some faraway continent.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.270
And they pushed the colonists out,

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:54.150
sometimes peacefully, but
most often with force.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:57.480
The Europeans had built
this insane global project

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.100
for more than 400 years,

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.906
and yet they saw it crumble
in a matter of decades.

00:20:01.906 --> 00:20:05.490
(tense orchestral music)

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:09.780
So, today, the map has
been severely redrawn.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:12.480
Former colonies are now
mostly independent countries.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:15.480
There are still a ton of
weird, idiosyncratic holdovers

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:16.500
from the colonial period.

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:17.880
I've talked about those many times

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.830
and I will continue to talk about them.

00:20:20.932 --> 00:20:23.432
(light music)

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:25.920
I wanna finish this up,

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:28.230
finish this video and
finish this series up

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.640
with my last thought here,

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.919
which is something that the
map doesn't tell us much about.

00:20:32.919 --> 00:20:36.240
(light music)

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.970
Even though all these
countries became independent

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.580
and they can claim their own
sovereignty, their own borders,

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:43.110
their colonizers are gone.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:44.160
They didn't actually.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:45.570
Not only were there loads of borders

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.700
that were literally drawn by colonizers,

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.850
I mean, see basically
all of my previous work.

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:51.683
But by the end of this

00:20:51.683 --> 00:20:55.080
it was the Europeans that
had tied the whole world up

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.240
into an interconnected system

00:20:57.240 --> 00:20:59.370
that still kind of echoed the old one.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:01.590
The Dutch invention of the
shareholder corporation

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:02.460
didn't go away.

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:04.377
Private companies didn't
suddenly stop looking

00:21:04.377 --> 00:21:06.480
for the same far off places

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.730
to find resources, to find labor,

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.610
to feed increasing demand
among their people back home.

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:13.920
And European rulers and their offspring

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.760
didn't stop using their big
metal guns and their technology

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.280
to get what they wanted In faraway lands.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.020
Occasionally talking to each other

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:24.390
and occasionally fighting with each other.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.000
Fighting to control
land, to control people,

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.490
to control ideas, and
perhaps most powerfully,

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:33.480
the idea that our enlightened
way was indeed the best way.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.730
- We will stand with
the new leaders of Iraq

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:37.950
as they establish a government of,

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:41.580
by, and for the Iraqi people.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.601
- Certainly it has not gone away.

00:21:43.601 --> 00:21:46.050
(light music)

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:47.760
And yet, if it were only that simple,

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:49.920
a simple narrative of good and bad,

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:51.780
greedy Europeans take over the world

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:53.190
and do anything to stay ahead,

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:55.080
that would be a lot easier in some ways,

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:56.100
but it's not.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:57.690
Europe taking over the world

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:00.690
has also thrust humanity
into an age of peace

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:03.300
and prosperity where people live longer,

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:05.010
suffer less in a lot of ways,

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:06.270
have more food to eat.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:07.650
I mean, the very moral lens

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:09.240
that you and I are using right now

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:11.940
to evaluate the good
and bad of this history,

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:14.400
that was a lens that was
cultivated and developed

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.410
by the same cultures that pillaged

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:18.990
and subjugated their
way around the planet.

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:21.120
These ideals of justice and equality

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.310
and human rights and representation,

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.800
social equality and self-determination,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.200
those ideas permeated the globe

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.750
alongside the colonizers who carved it up.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:34.140
And yet, it was this conquest
that put these people

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:35.880
on top of the whole system

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:39.420
giving us the power and the advantage,

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.115
the default power holders in our world.

00:22:42.115 --> 00:22:45.532
(light orchestral music)

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:51.030
These three parts have been a story

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:53.670
of how an isolated
group of farming people,

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.230
some of them my ancestors,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.120
left their shores to explore,

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.430
discovering a vast world that eventually

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:01.080
they would find a way to control.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.060
And in the process, setting the rules

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:04.503
for how things work today.

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:07.920
What's slightly scary to me about this

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.770
is how easy it is to look
back on this whole history

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:12.540
and feel like it was gonna happen

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:13.620
this way no matter what,

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:14.550
that it was inevitable.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.560
That of course Europeans
took over the world.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:17.610
They were always more adept,

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.200
they were bound to control the planet.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:20.400
But if there's anything I've learned

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:23.340
diving into this broad tour
through European imperialism

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.070
is that this idea is just hindsight bias.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:29.130
This didn't happen because
of some superior DNA

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.580
or because God wanted these
people to take over the world,

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:34.950
but rather it happened to
because a bunch of people

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:36.690
happened to be at the right place

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:38.610
that allowed them to start a chain

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.290
of millions of little decisions

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.240
that pushed them to do whatever they could

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.580
to procure more and more resources.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:47.220
They got ahead because
of lucky circumstances.

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:48.990
And yet today in our modern world,

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:52.200
we continue to do whatever
we need to to stay ahead

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.030
while simultaneously believing

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:56.216
that it was always gonna happen this way.

00:23:56.216 --> 00:23:58.716
(light music)

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:14.883
It's over.

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:16.793
It's over.

00:24:16.793 --> 00:24:18.270
I mean, the series is over,

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:20.400
but the year is over.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:21.900
I deserve this.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:24.120
Not only is it the end
of the Europe series,

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:25.230
it's the end of the year

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:27.210
and what a year it has been.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.240
By the way, this video is
not sponsored by anyone,

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.560
probably because we're in a recession.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.420
And so I'm gonna take this time

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:36.780
to just reflect a little bit
on what this year has meant

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:38.760
for me as a storyteller and a journalist

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:42.180
and for our team and what
we are gonna do next.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:43.980
I think for me, the big
thing I learned this year

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.330
was that we can build a big operation.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:49.680
Like for a long time it was just me

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.390
kind of pushing buttons on a laptop

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:52.740
and like making stories.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:54.900
And this year, it actually became a team.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.730
We now have lots and lots of people

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.490
making really, really cool
stuff, a very talented team.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.580
And we're doing journalism on
a level that we've never done.

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:06.420
We go really deep on every fact
that we say on this channel,

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:07.830
and that requires a lot of work.

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:09.270
Sometimes that feels really tiresome,

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:11.460
but a lot of the time that feels exciting.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:14.820
That we on YouTube can be
doing like hardcore journalism

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.440
and fun stuff too, obviously.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:18.870
We got to do a few in the
field documentaries this year,

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:20.130
which was really amazing.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:23.790
I went to Paris and Korea and Switzerland.

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:25.890
We printed a lot of amazing maps

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.560
and showed you what maps
were like over the years

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:29.820
and what they were used for.

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.640
Oh, and my hair got really long

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:34.080
'cause I decided for the
first time in my life

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:34.980
not to cut it,

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:38.490
and that's making my
whole no shampoo thing

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:39.690
a little more complicated.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:41.280
Maybe I'll make a video about that.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:44.403
Anyway.
(romantic music)

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:48.810
I eat a lot of Doritos.

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:50.760
Like a lot of Doritos.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:52.230
Like I really like Doritos,

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:53.730
even after I know how they're made.

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:55.890
But in my mind, this is
kind of just the beginning.

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.360
Like I want this to be the beginning.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:58.957
I want like four years from
now to look back and be like,

00:25:58.957 --> 00:25:59.790
"Oh yeah, 2022.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:01.770
That was the year that we
really started to ramp up."

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:02.700
And that gets me to the thing

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:04.770
that I'm really excited about
that we launched this year,

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:07.170
which is a community on
Patreon called The Newsroom.

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:09.440
A place where people like you support us

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:10.620
in what we're trying to do here

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:11.640
and allow us to grow.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:14.730
More and more, and especially
during economic downturn

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.410
and unpredictability,

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:19.620
this work that we do relies
on community support,

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.290
on people like you showing
up and supporting us.

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:24.330
And it's our Newsroom
supporters that are allowing us

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:26.340
to do better work, to do more of it,

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:27.750
and I'm very grateful for that.

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.873
In addition to supporting
independent journalism,

00:26:32.348 --> 00:26:33.181
Newsroom members also get
access to a bunch of cool stuff,

00:26:33.181 --> 00:26:34.230
like a behind the scenes vlog,

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:35.520
an extra video every month.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.500
There's a lot of really talented,

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:38.970
interesting people behind the scenes.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.340
And every month you get
a vlog where you see

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:42.330
how it's all done.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:45.210
You get access to music
from our composer Tom Fox.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:47.250
You get access to my scripts.

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:50.160
Like occasionally we do
Q&amp;As where we get to chat.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.380
I do polls where I get story ideas.

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:54.810
It's become a really cool
community of supporters

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:57.600
and just a kind of a sounding
board for cool ideas.

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:58.680
Oh, and here's a big one.

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:01.080
We've started publishing
our videos over on Nebula,

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:02.850
which is a creator-owned platform

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:05.520
where you can get our videos a week early.

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.680
There's a link in my description
that explains all that.

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:08.940
If you wanna support the channel

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:10.680
and you're like a production person,

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:12.600
then we have LUTS and Presets,

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:14.730
which help us color our videos and photos.

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:16.380
And if you don't wanna support the channel

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:18.030
and you just wanna keep watching,

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:18.990
that's fine, too.

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:21.150
I'm not here to tell you to give me money.

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:23.730
I'm here to make cool stories

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.160
and I hope that you're
learning from these videos

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.200
and I appreciate you being
here in the first place.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:32.220
So, let's raise a glass
to a wonderful year

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:35.760
of learning and growth
and amazing stories,

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:39.570
and look forward to a 2023 that I hope

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:41.820
is gonna be full of even more growth.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:44.970
Not only growth in the number
of people that are here,

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:47.730
but also a growth in our desire

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:49.830
to like understand this world,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:53.010
to understand the story
of how we all got here

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:54.660
and how it works today.

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:56.820
I will continue to be
the curious storyteller

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:58.560
who tries to bring those stories to you

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:00.210
in the best way possible.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:02.490
I appreciate all of those
who are here supporting,

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:07.200
and cheers to a great 2023 of curiosity

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:08.370
and a lot of maps.

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:09.990
Bye everyone.

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:13.073
